  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nAn upper level trough will be weakening but will remain over the \neastern CONUS as surface high pressure to our north and northwest \ndrifts slowly east through Tuesday. This will provide us with a \ndry days and comfortably low relative humidity values this \nafternoon and again Tuesday. Some patchy fog may develop again in \nthe late night hours tonight mainly near rivers and lakes. \n\nBy mid week the surface high will be off to our east and northeast, \nallowing low level flow to become more southwesterly and moisture to \nincrease. Short wave energy may have enough moisture and instability \nto work with for a few showers and thunderstorms Wednesday, \nespecially south and mountains. By Thursday into Thursday night, \nmodels show additioanl short wave energy aloft and a weak cold front \npushing into our area. This will likely result in the period with \nthe highest coverage of showers and storms. The details such as \ntiming of the front/main forcing will matter and are still \nuncertain, so we will have to wait until we get closer to see about \nthe potential for any strong to severe storms. Right now the \nensemble data joint probabilities for at least 500 J/kg of CAPE and \n> 30kt bulk shear are minimal at best and the chance for organized \nsevere storms looks quite low. \n\nUpper ridging and surface high pressure will build in by the \nweekend, with most areas expected to be dry. The NBM just has some \nlow chances for afternoon showers and storms mainly in the mountains \nboth Saturday and Sunday. With the ridging building in we will also \nsee temperatures trending back above normal during the weekend into \nMonday.",
